2. Paste your useDebounce hook. How does cleanup prevent unnecessary API calls?
import {
    useEffect,
    useState,
} from "react";

export default function useDebounce<T>(
    value: T,
    delay = 300
) {
    const [
        debouncedValue,
        setDebouncedValue,
    ] = useState<T>(value);

    useEffect(() => {
        const timer = setTimeout(() => {
            setDebouncedValue(value);
        }, delay);

        return () => {
            clearTimeout(timer);
        };
    }, [value, delay]);

    return debouncedValue;
}
The useDebounce hook delays updating the final value for 300 milliseconds.
For example, when a user searches for an employee and types the word Rahul, the value changes several times:
R → Ra → Rah → Rahu → Rahul
Without debouncing, the application could make an API request for every character typed.
The cleanup function solves this problem:
return () => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
};
Every time the user types another character before the 300 milliseconds are completed, React runs the cleanup function from the previous effect. The previous timer is cancelled before it can update debouncedValue.
Therefore, only when the user stops typing for 300 milliseconds does the final timer complete and update debouncedValue.
The Employees page listens to debouncedSearch, not the original search value. As a result, the API is called only after the user pauses typing, which prevents unnecessary API requests and improves application performance.
